# LaTeX in Markdown

When writing math in markdown (chat replies, notes, writeups):

## Cursor chat: single `$` does not render

In Cursor chat preview, `$...$` inline math shows as raw text. Only `$$...$$` renders.
**Never use single `$` delimiters in chat responses.** Use `$$` for all math, even short symbols.

## Do not use

- **Single-dollar inline math** — `$G_t$`, `$R_{t+1}$` (raw in Cursor chat)
- **Blockquotes containing math** — `>` lines with `$$...$$` inside
- **Backticks around math** — `` `$$\delta_t$$` ``
- **Bold/italic wrapping math** — `**$$\delta_t$$**`
- **Bare underscores outside math** — write `$$S_t$$`, not `S_t` (markdown treats `_` as emphasis)

## Use instead

- **All math in chat:** `$$` on its own lines, with blank lines above and below:

  $$
  \delta_t \doteq R_{t+1} + \gamma V(S_{t+1}) - V(S_t)
  $$

- **Short symbols in prose:** put the symbol on its own `$$` line between sentences, not mid-sentence with `$`.

  Monte Carlo uses the full return. The TD target is:

  $$
  R_{t+1} + \gamma V(S_{t+1})
  $$

- **Quoted passages with math:** plain paragraphs, not `>` blockquotes.
- **Lists with math:** normal paragraph under the list item, not nested blockquotes.

## Committed markdown files

GitHub and some viewers accept `$...$` inline. Cursor chat does not. Prefer `$$` blocks in files you will discuss in chat; use `$` inline only in files meant solely for external viewers.

## Examples

Bad (chat — single `$`):

Monte Carlo uses $G_t$; TD uses $R_{t+1} + \gamma V(S_{t+1})$.

Good (chat):

Monte Carlo uses the return $$G_t$$. The one-step TD target is:

$$
R_{t+1} + \gamma V(S_{t+1})
$$

Bad (chat — math in blockquote):

> TD error: $$\delta_t = r + \gamma V(s') - V(s)$$

Good (chat):

TD error:

$$
\delta_t = r + \gamma V(s') - V(s)
$$
